{"crc":"e799b442","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"Looking at the issue, the problem is that when `__all__` is an empty list, autodoc ignores it and shows all members instead of showing none. The fix needs to be in the `get_object_members` function in `sphinx/ext/autodoc/importer.py` where it checks for `__all__`.\n\nLet me examine the relevant code in the `__init__.py` file to find where `__all__` is handled:\n\nThe issue is in the `ModuleDocumenter.get_object_members` method. When `__all__` is an empty list, it should return an empty dict, but currently it's being treated as falsy and falling through to the default behavior.\n\nLet me search for the relevant code:\n\n# path: sphinx/ext/autodoc/__init__.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def get_object_members(self, want_all: bool) -> Tuple[bool, ObjectMembers]:\n        members = get_object_members(self.object, self.objpath, self.get_attr, self.analyzer)\n        if not want_all:\n            if not self.options.ignore_module_all:\n                if hasattr(self.object, '__all__'):\n                    all_members = self.object.__all__\n                    if all_members is not None:\n                        ret = []\n                        for name in all_members:\n                            if name in members:\n                                ret.append(members[name])\n                        return False, ret\n            # implicit module members\n            return False, [(m.name, m.value) for m in members.values()\n                           if not m.name.startswith('_')]\n        else:\n            return False, [(m.name, m.value) for m in members.values()]\n=======\n    def get_object_members(self, want_all: bool) -> Tuple[bool, ObjectMembers]:\n        members = get_object_members(self.object, self.objpath, self.get_attr, self.analyzer)\n        if not want_all:\n            if not self.options.ignore_module_all:\n                if hasattr(self.object, '__all__'):\n                    all_members = sel